WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.095 --> 00:00:12.880
NARRATOR: We speed across
M87, A gigantic galaxy

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.610
55 million light
years from Earth.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:25.755
At its heart lies a supermassive
black hole, M87 star.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.990
It is the first and only
black hole ever photographed.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.700
We want to find out how
M87 star grew so large,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.150
what lies inside, and how
it controls the galaxy.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.220
5,000 light years out from
the supermassive black hole,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.760
we get our first sign
of the danger ahead.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:01.470
We see giant holes carved out
of the galaxy, starless void's

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.671
thousands of light years wide.

00:01:04.671 --> 00:01:08.160
PAUL M. SUTTER: As we approach
we can see that wreckage

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.290
littered around the vicinity.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.780
It's like entering
the lair of the dragon

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.580
and seeing the bones of all the
explorers who came before you.

00:01:18.292 --> 00:01:22.140
NARRATOR: What cataclysmic
force tore these giant cavities

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.385
in the galactic gas clouds?

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.480
As we fly next to
a brilliant shaft

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:37.150
of energy thousands of
light years from M87 star,

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:37.950
we get a clue.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.420
It's a deadly
stream of radiation

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:45.885
shooting out across the galaxy.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.620
A jet.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.170
 This jet looks
like a searchlight,

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.190
or a beam from a lighthouse.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.450
You're seeing this
monumental thing

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.720
screaming out of the black
hole, blasting out radiation.

00:02:02.456 --> 00:02:06.520
MAX TEGMARK: When I first saw
a photo of a jet I was like,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.400
whoa, am I like, misreading
the scale of this image?

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.130
Because there was
this crazy "Star Trek"

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:13.870
like beam just coming out.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:19.110
NARRATOR: In 1918
American astronomer,

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:24.030
Heber Curtis described the jets
as a curious, straight ray.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.930
A century later,
observatory images reveal

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.580
they pulsate with energy.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.330
PAUL M. SUTTER: The
images show knot's,

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.520
and clumps in these jets.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.120
They show that it's just
not smooth and nice,

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.870
that there's been a history
of violence inside this jet.

00:02:48.225 --> 00:02:51.070
NARRATOR: This
violent energy pushes

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.450
the knots along the beams.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.545
The knots reveal the
speed of the jets.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.710
It's like looking at
a fast moving train.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:07.890
Rail cars of the same color
blur into one continuous image.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.470
But, different colored cars
stand out against the others.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.415
It's the same with the
knots moving along the jets.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.930
 So, we can figure out how
fast the jets are really moving

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.760
by looking at knots
of material coming

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.130
out from near the black hole.

00:03:29.345 --> 00:03:32.060
NARRATOR: When astronomers
measured the speed of two

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.905
knots, they got a big surprise.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:39.080
 One is moving at 2.4
times the speed of light,

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.980
and the other is moving over
6 times faster than light.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:44.450
How could this possibly be?

00:03:45.875 --> 00:03:49.020
 As weird as the physics
around a black hole is,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.170
that's not actually happening,
nor is it allowed to happen.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.060
PAUL M. SUTTER:
Nothing can actually go

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.480
faster than the speed of light.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:58.080
So obviously, we're
missing something here.

00:03:59.436 --> 00:04:04.130
NARRATOR: The knots may seem
to break the speed of light.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:07.392
But, the universe is
just playing with us.

00:04:07.392 --> 00:04:10.210
 It's really just a
consequence of the fact

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.460
that a lot of this
jet is pointed

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:15.810
toward us, pointed partially
toward the observer on Earth.

00:04:17.478 --> 00:04:20.209
MAX TEGMARK: That in a sense,
the sort of optical illusion,

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:22.064
tricks you into thinking
it's moving faster.

00:04:23.671 --> 00:04:26.180
NARRATOR: It's a simple
trick of the light.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.420
A bit like the way a
spoon in a glass of water

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.790
looks bent, and distorted.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.520
The impossibly fast
speed of the jet

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.322
is just an illusion
of perspective.

00:04:37.322 --> 00:04:39.140
PAUL M. SUTTER: From
our perspective,

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:40.780
it looks like the
whole thing is moving

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.340
towards us faster than light.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.430
But really, it's just cruising
along very, very fast.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.380
MAX TEGMARK: The
Jets aren't actually

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:49.640
breaking the laws of physics.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:54.620
They're pushing up against it,
they're going at 99.999995%

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.245
the speed of light.

00:04:56.245 --> 00:05:00.320
 Imagine the energies
necessary to accelerate

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.630
this entire jet to that speed.

00:05:04.106 --> 00:05:07.720
NARRATOR: So, what could
produce enough energy

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:11.950
to blast jets across the galaxy
at close to the speed of light?

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.910
There is a clue far ahead.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:21.220
The Jets shoot out from a
tiny, brightly glowing object.

00:05:22.502 --> 00:05:24.800
 This is where things go nuts.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.510
This is the center
of the action.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:30.260
This is where the
real stuff happens.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.140
NARRATOR: A ring
of super hot gas,

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:36.800
and dust whirls around the
supermassive black hole.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.940
It's called the accretion disk.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.480
And it shines a billion
times brighter than the sun.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:51.910
CHIARA MINGARELLI: If you had a
ringside seat next to M87 star,

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:55.050
you would probably be
fried very, very fast.

00:05:59.604 --> 00:06:01.990
 But, if you were some, you
know, magical being and could

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.120
survive anything, and
if you had, you know,

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:07.870
million SPF sunscreen, and
really, really great sunglasses

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:12.010
what you would see is this
enormously bright vortex

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.631
of gas swirling this dark void.

00:06:15.631 --> 00:06:17.830
NARRATOR: This
bright vortex spins

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:20.500
around the
supermassive black hole

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:23.351
at over two million
miles an hour.

00:06:23.351 --> 00:06:24.730
 So, there's a
tremendous amount

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:27.670
of friction as material
moving slower and faster

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.120
rubs against each other.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.640
That's what's
heating the disk up,

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.320
and that's what's
causing it to glow.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:38.070
NARRATOR: Scientists think
that the intense energy

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:40.860
of the accretion disk is
the source of the jets.

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:45.320
The hot swirling gas
and dust produces

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:46.745
powerful magnetic fields.

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:52.250
As the disk spins, it twists
up the magnetic fields

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:54.410
at the poles of the black hole.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:55.265
Energy builds.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:59.060
Finally, the
magnetic fields can't

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:00.710
contain the energy any longer.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:07.190
They snap, and blast the
jets out into the galaxy.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.680
Even many light years
away on the ship,

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:12.340
we can see this violent
release of energy.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:17.600
It's like the universe's
biggest fireworks display.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:23.050
Two jets streaking out
of M87 stars poles.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:29.650
One shooting away into the
distance, the other racing

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:30.550
past our ship.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:37.416
We're at a safe distance,
other things are not.

00:07:37.416 --> 00:07:39.590
 So, when these
jets shoot outward

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:41.010
from this supermassive
black hole,

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:42.440
they don't shoot
outward into nothing.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:46.220
 If a jet hits a gas
cloud, it annihilates it.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:49.190
It just punches a
hole right through it.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:52.280
GRANT TREMBLAY: It's like a
train going down a snowy track,

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:53.080
right?

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.110
The gas is like the snow, and
the jets are like this freight

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:57.800
train plowing across it.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.410
NARRATOR: But here, a
freight train traveling

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:03.970
at close to the speed of light.

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:13.020
Smashing into clouds
of gas, lighting

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:19.031
our way to M87 Star as we
follow the trail of destruction.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.120
There is evidence of
similar destruction

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:26.644
across the universe.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:32.350
In the Cygnus A galaxy,
supermassive black hole jets

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:35.080
have caused damage
on a colossal scale.

00:08:36.896 --> 00:08:38.730
GRANT TREMBLAY: In
many ways, Cygnus A is

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.289
like a cosmic shooting gallery.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:44.160
You see this crime scene,
this beautiful mess.

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:45.900
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: So,
when this jet comes out

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:50.160
of the nucleus of Cygnus A, it's
going to encounter gas clouds.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.410
At that point,
shock waves set up.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.110
And this jet just rips
right through this material,

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:57.270
sending shock waves
in every direction,

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.145
creating absolute chaos.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:03.570
 It's hard to believe
how much devastation

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:05.100
these jets can cause.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:08.550
They're punching a hole in the
gas 100,000 light years wide.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:11.070
I mean, that's, that's the
scale of an entire galaxy.

00:09:17.579 --> 00:09:21.840
NARRATOR: As we head towards
the center of the M87 galaxy,

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.550
we enter hostile territory.

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:28.500
The closer to the supermassive
black hole we travel,

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:30.802
the more dangerous it gets.

00:09:30.802 --> 00:09:34.760
 As we approach the
central core of M87,

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.560
we start to feel it.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:39.230
All this energy, all
this ferociousness

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:40.820
is powered by that black hole.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:45.290
NARRATOR: Intense winds
start to buff at the ship.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.480
They push away vital gas,
quenching star birth.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:10:00.460
Could these winds end up
killing the galaxy, and M87 Star

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:02.010
itself?

